WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.086 --> 00:00:04.586
(upbeat brass band music)

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:33.120
- (indistinct speech)

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:33.953
- Thank you.

00:00:33.953 --> 00:00:36.946
- We gotta follow the boss.

00:00:36.946 --> 00:00:38.560
(audience chuckles)

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:39.843
- Hello everyone.

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:42.343
And welcome.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.500
So I am thrilled to announce

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:52.140
that the next Dean of Yale
College is Pericles Lewis,

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.890
the Douglas Tracy Smith Professor

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:56.735
of Comparative Literature

00:00:56.735 --> 00:00:58.980
(audience applauding)

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:00.828
and Professor of English.

00:01:00.828 --> 00:01:03.710
(audience applauding)

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:08.710
Great. Thank you all for joining
us for this announcement.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.110
Thanks to the Yale,

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:13.610
is that the Precision Marching Band,

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:15.610
the concert band random?

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:16.443
Okay, I got it.

00:01:16.443 --> 00:01:20.006
Thank you, Tom Duffy, for
assembling a band for us today.

00:01:20.006 --> 00:01:21.115
Thank you so much.

00:01:21.115 --> 00:01:23.940
(audience applauding)

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:28.790
Pericles is joined by his
family, his wife Sheila Hayre,

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:32.600
their daughter, Maya,
and their son, Siddhartha

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.420
couldn't join us today

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:36.913
but he is watching on zoom, I understand.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.330
Sheila is a graduate
of the Yale Law School

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:45.610
and currently teaches at
the Quinnipiac Law School.

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:49.890
Siddhartha graduated from
Yale College two days ago

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:51.673
with a BS in Mathematics.

00:01:51.673 --> 00:01:55.280
(audience applauding)

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:56.810
We congratulate him.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:59.150
And Maya is a Yale College sophomore

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:02.236
majoring in English and Biology.

00:02:02.236 --> 00:02:05.403
(audience applauding)

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.800
So many of you know,

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.910
Professor Lewis is an
internationally recognized expert

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:15.863
on the qualities and purposes
of liberal of education.

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:18.250
He's been a talent.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:20.640
He is a talented faculty member,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.820
been committed teacher
and educator here at Yale

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:28.170
since he joined the faculty in 1998,

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:33.170
and has been deeply involved
in university life ever since.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.630
In addition to teaching
and writing and editing,

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:38.440
Professor Lewis has served now

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.260
as the Vice President for Global Strategy

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:46.260
and as a vice provost
for Academic Initiatives.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:49.520
Among as many contributions
in those roles,

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.420
he worked with the Yale
School of Public Health

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:56.070
to launch an online
master's degree program

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.320
that we worked with the Jackson Institute

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:02.450
to create a new master's degree
program in Public Policy.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.550
He's worked with the deans of Medicine,

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.610
Nursing and Public Health

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:10.710
to establish the Yale
Institute for Global Health,

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:14.480
and his role also included oversight

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.080
over the McMillan Center,
the Yale Center Beijing,

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.660
the Yale Young Global Scholars Program,

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:24.980
and many other departments and centers.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:29.980
But his work in the
education of students at Yale

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:33.300
goes back decades.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.830
He was the Director of
Undergraduate Studies

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:41.627
in Comp Lit back in
2000, from 2000 to 2006,

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:45.780
and he helped integrate the two majors

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:48.240
that the department used to sponsor.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.460
He updated the curriculum in 2005.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:57.460
He worked on the undergraduate
curriculum as a member

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:04.260
and later as Chair of the Yale
College Committee on Majors,

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.360
and in his scholarly life, he
works on literary modernism.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.260
He's the author of three books

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.830
on the social and intellectual contexts

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:18.110
of modern literature.

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:20.380
And right now he is
working on a manuscript

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:24.720
on the role of liberal education
in developing character,

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:28.300
fostering community, preparing
young people for citizenship,

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:31.883
encouraging the pursuit of knowledge.

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.270
Now I think many of you know,

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:38.493
I'm realizing you're
all skewed to one side.

00:04:38.493 --> 00:04:42.140
I'm getting you all only in
one hemisphere of my brain

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:43.750
at the moment,

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:48.750
but between 2012 and 2017,

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.080
Professor Lewis left Yale

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.350
to serve as the Founding
President of Yale-NUS College.

00:04:57.370 --> 00:05:02.280
He quickly established himself
as an international authority

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.330
on global arts and sciences education.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:07.260
He oversaw the development

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:10.290
of the Yale-NUS college curriculum,

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:13.520
which was a very innovative curriculum.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.370
He recruited diverse groups of students

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:20.600
and faculty and staff to
work at Yale-NUS College

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:24.630
and as a result of this foundational work,

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:27.610
Yale-NUS College did what
we hoped it would do,

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:32.370
which was inspire innovation
in undergraduate education

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:36.750
first at the National
University of Singapore

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:38.540
then throughout Singapore,

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:40.610
then through other institutions in Asia

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:45.460
and ultimately influence what
we did here domestically,

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:48.653
especially in Yale College.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:52.190
Now, some of you in this room have worked

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:55.060
with Pericles over the past two years

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:58.150
especially on sustaining Yale's teaching

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:01.523
and research missions during COVID.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:03.870
That has not been easy

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:07.310
and I wish I could thank individually

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:10.950
so many of you in this room
who played important roles

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.470
in getting us through the last two years,

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.010
those roles are not over yet,

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:17.270
I wish I could say they were.

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:19.970
But Pericles, as you know

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:23.460
was a very important advisor on COVID 19

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:27.953
because he chaired the
Academic Continuity Committee

00:06:27.953 --> 00:06:31.407
which is essentially addressed,

00:06:31.407 --> 00:06:33.650
"How do we keep going?

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:36.540
How do we keep pursuing research?

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:40.460
How do we keep pursuing
education and learning

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:44.350
despite the challenges of COVID."

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:47.690
He was also a member of the
Operating Policy Committee

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:51.170
which translated
recommendations we were getting

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:55.330
from Yale's unbelievable
experts in the health sciences,

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:56.820
particularly modeling going on

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.200
in the School of Public Health.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:06:59.590
How do you translate that

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:02.350
into actions we can take on campus?

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:04.290
So he worked in partnership

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:07.050
with faculty and staff and students,

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:11.997
and figured out how and when
we could welcome students back

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:15.010
to a safe campus environment

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:20.260
and how we could educate them
both remotely and in person.

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:24.060
He oversaw the Poorvu Center
for Teaching and Learning.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:26.880
As you know, they played a critical role.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:29.240
Their staff played a critical role

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:34.210
in helping us convert to
online and hybrid education.

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:38.030
During that time he guided
the university planning

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:40.960
for the opening of the Schwarzman Center.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.690
We are now in Commons of
the Schwarzman Center,

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:48.870
this beautiful room that has
been preserved historically

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:51.830
but in ways that are far more functional

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:54.550
than it used to be down below.

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:56.310
I hope virtually everyone

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:58.820
in this room has seen
those beautiful spaces.

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:03.440
And of course, behind that
wall, all kinds of new resources

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:08.040
for students and the
larger Yale community.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:09.820
He contributed to the planning

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:13.110
of the new Yale Jackson
School for Global Affairs

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:17.403
during this period of time as well.

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:22.597
So on July 1st,

00:08:22.597 --> 00:08:26.270
Pericles will step down from
all of those other roles

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:30.090
except for his oversight
of the Schwarzman Center,

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:35.090
and assume the role of
Dean of Yale College.

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:39.500
We will absorb many of
his previous functions

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:42.140
within the Provost's Office

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:46.443
and we're working that out
now and throughout the summer.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:51.630
There is no doubt, however,

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:55.610
that with his international experience,

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:58.830
we will still turn to Pericles for advice

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:01.890
on all kinds of issues

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:06.300
during these geo politically
complicated times.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:10.230
He will though focus on his partnerships

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:11.610
with other university leaders,

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:14.240
with the faculty, with the staff,

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.510
guiding the curriculum,
the intellectual life

00:09:18.710 --> 00:09:22.310
of Yale College, student
affairs in Yale College

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:25.920
and of course with the heads
of the residential colleges

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:28.600
and the deans of residential college,

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:33.113
the experience of life within
the residential communities.

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:36.040
In doing so, he will build

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:39.060
on Dean Marvin Chun's
immense accomplishments

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:40.850
over the last five years

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:44.370
for which we are incredibly grateful,

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:47.080
I've more to say about that in a moment,

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:51.240
ensuring that an undergraduate
education at Yale

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.840
is unsurpassed,

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:57.460
unsurpassed by any other
liberal arts college

00:09:57.460 --> 00:10:01.480
sitting in the middle of
a research university.

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:06.480
It will be and continue to be
a model for the entire world.

00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:10.650
Let me take a moment to thank

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:14.230
the Search Advisory Committee members

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:17.550
chaired by Professor Margaret Clark,

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:21.800
who is also the head of Trumbull College

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:24.810
and Included Professor Aimee Meredith Cox,

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:28.470
Dean Christina Ferando,
Professor Alan Gerber,

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:31.760
former Deputy Dean, Joe Gordon,

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:36.760
Professor Tina Lu, Myan
Chant from the class of 2023,

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:38.910
Professor David Faso,

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:42.140
and my chief of staff, Susan Gibbons

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:43.730
who staffed the committee.

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:47.513
They did enormous amounts of
outreach to the Yale community.

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:51.180
They provided thoughtful recommendations

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:55.940
and a very exciting short
list of potential candidates.

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:02.240
Finally, finally, my heartfelt gratitude

00:11:03.370 --> 00:11:07.350
to my partner in crime
here, Dean Marvin Chun.

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:08.424
We are...

00:11:08.424 --> 00:11:11.591
(audience applauding)

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:26.750
Dean Chun and I, have been colleagues

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:29.960
in the Psychology department for decades.

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:33.060
I was involved in hiring him

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:35.180
in the Psychology department twice

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:39.840
as well as his wife, Woo-kyoung.

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:44.840
And one couldn't have been
blessed with a better colleague,

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:51.070
a more wonderful friend,

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:56.070
and a Dean more committed
to the life and education

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:00.300
of our undergraduates.

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:02.590
You've been an outstanding leader

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:05.710
for Yale College for the past five years.

00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:07.410
I know you share my excitement

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:11.410
about Professor Pericles
Lewis' appointment,

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:16.040
and before turning the podium
over to Professor Lewis,

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:18.810
I want to invite Dean Chun

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:20.260
to say a few words to us.

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:21.093
Once again,

00:12:21.093 --> 00:12:22.590
thank you all for being here

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:25.333
and thank you for all
that you do for Yale.

00:12:25.333 --> 00:12:26.910
(audience applauding)

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:27.743
Dean Chun.

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:36.080
- Good afternoon.

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:37.960
Please excuse my mask,

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:41.430
I'm following close contact protocols.

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:43.130
I'm wondering where I got exposed.

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:47.220
We just had Commencement
for 20,000 people.

00:12:48.290 --> 00:12:51.830
I'm delighted to welcome Professor Lewis

00:12:51.830 --> 00:12:54.590
to the best job in academia.

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:58.010
I've long admired his
contributions to Yale

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:00.180
and his achievements as
the Founding President

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:02.390
of Yale-NUS College.

00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:05.830
Because I was involved in
the planning of Yale-NUS

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:07.830
and served on their governing board,

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:10.440
I'm in a pretty unique
position to be in awe

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:13.100
of what he had accomplished there.

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:16.620
Professor Lewis had led Yale-NUS College

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:18.430
to become one of the most innovative

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:21.200
liberal arts colleges around the globe

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:25.030
with a curriculum that
is global and inclusive,

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:27.800
a residential life that
successfully incorporated

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:31.030
the best features of
Yale's residential colleges

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:34.180
while adapting to Singapore context.

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:38.207
If you want, you can Google
"Yale-NUS College sky garden."

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:42.540
They have a faculty committed
to collaborative teaching,

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:45.920
a staff dedicated to
both a rigorous education

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:47.460
and student wellness,

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:50.310
and an internationally
diverse student body

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:52.710
that like our students at Yale,

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:54.880
are sought after by top institutions

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:58.570
for their love of learning
and care for others.

00:13:58.570 --> 00:14:01.500
I went into detail about
them because I'm excited

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:05.350
for what professor Lewis
will bring to Yale College.

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:08.350
He begins his term as
a Dean of Yale College

00:14:08.350 --> 00:14:10.100
with more administrative experience

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:12.500
than I had starting in this role,

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:13.520
and more than I have now

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:16.380
even after completing a five year term.

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:17.910
So frankly, I'm very flattered

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:22.113
to see such an academic
superstar leader as my successor.

00:14:23.140 --> 00:14:27.200
In turn, I'm proud and happy
to be passing on a college

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:30.270
that is flourishing as best as one can

00:14:30.270 --> 00:14:33.800
after a challenging
two years for everyone.

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:37.100
The Yale College Dean's
Office is more collaborative,

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:40.370
proactive and resilient than before

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:43.450
because that is how the
faculty staff and students

00:14:43.450 --> 00:14:44.333
have all been.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:47.150
I'm grateful to President Salovey

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:49.650
for entrusting me with this role.

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:52.880
And I thank him for
supporting Yale College and me

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:55.960
so strongly throughout my entire term.

00:14:55.960 --> 00:14:58.890
Every significant request I made

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:01.370
such as the elimination
of billed student effort

00:15:01.370 --> 00:15:06.020
and financial aid and the
Camp Yale orientation flip,

00:15:06.020 --> 00:15:08.223
he has granted with enthusiasm.

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:12.300
I also thank Provost Scott Strobel.

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:15.240
He has provided all the
guidance and resources needed

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:17.710
to manage through the pandemic,

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:21.220
culminating in the renaissance
of in-person campus life

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:23.723
and two glorious commencement weekends.

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:26.690
Provost Strobel has
positioned Yale College

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:29.310
for greater success in the years ahead,

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:32.650
building on former
Provost Ben Polak's legacy

00:15:32.650 --> 00:15:36.530
by approving significant
enhancements to financial aid,

00:15:36.530 --> 00:15:38.760
residential life, student life,

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:41.963
and of course, academic
programs and facilities.

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:44.555
It's kinda like starting
with a blank check.

00:15:44.555 --> 00:15:46.120
(Professor Pericles chuckles)

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:47.380
Much of the college's work

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:49.990
is powered by our distinguished
faculty colleagues

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:53.740
led by Faculty of Arts and
Sciences, Dean Tamar Gendler,

00:15:53.740 --> 00:15:56.040
School of Engineering
and Applied Science Dean,

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:59.460
Jeff Brock and FAS Steering Group.

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:01.130
Yale College also works closely

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:03.680
with the Graduate School
led by Lynn Cooley,

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:05.890
and we are grateful for
our academic partnerships

00:16:05.890 --> 00:16:07.760
with the professional schools

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:09.840
where our undergraduate
students are fortunate

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:12.533
to take classes and pursue majors.

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:14.930
I'd like to remind everyone here

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:18.470
that Yale College depends
on the entire university

00:16:18.470 --> 00:16:21.480
and so I am grateful for
having worked regularly

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:25.180
with the Yale Alumni
Association, Yale Athletics,

00:16:25.180 --> 00:16:29.490
Chaplain's office, Facilities
and Grounds, General Counsel,

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:31.730
Yale Health, Yale Hospitality,

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:36.550
Information Technology Services,
Human Resources Operations,

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:39.800
Police and Public Security,
Office of Public Affairs,

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:41.540
Secretary's office,

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:42.970
and also other key offices

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:45.550
that Professor Lewis
has supervised already,

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:48.130
the Registrar's Office, the Poorvu Center,

00:16:48.130 --> 00:16:50.250
Conferences and Events, Global Strategy,

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:51.633
and the Schwarzman Center.

00:16:53.370 --> 00:16:56.130
We have incredibly caring
alumni parents and friends

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:57.560
who support our mission

00:16:57.560 --> 00:16:59.520
with inspiring generosity,

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:01.170
and their gifts are managed wisely

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:02.860
by the three offices

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:05.753
of Development, Investments, and Finance.

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:08.570
I believe that Yale has the most vibrant

00:17:08.570 --> 00:17:11.080
residential college
communities in the world

00:17:11.080 --> 00:17:13.790
led by prominent heads,
deans, and staff members

00:17:13.790 --> 00:17:15.870
devoted to our students.

00:17:15.870 --> 00:17:17.970
I will always be proud and grateful

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:20.480
to have served as head of Berkeley college

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:24.050
as much as I am to have served
as Dean of Yale College.

00:17:24.050 --> 00:17:26.810
Each year, the Yale College Dean's office

00:17:26.810 --> 00:17:29.210
trains over 200 student leaders.

00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:32.470
First year counselors, peer
liaisons, peer mentors,

00:17:32.470 --> 00:17:35.730
communication and consent
educators, committee members,

00:17:35.730 --> 00:17:36.900
and student staff members

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:38.883
in our offices and cultural centers.

00:17:39.890 --> 00:17:43.130
And working independently
but in close collaboration,

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:46.090
Yale College Council
is an essential partner

00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:49.323
to help the Dean understand
and support student needs.

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:52.510
The Yale College Dean's office itself

00:17:52.510 --> 00:17:55.950
has over 230 outstanding staff members

00:17:55.950 --> 00:17:59.120
across the offices of Academic Affairs,

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:02.550
Admissions and Financial
Aid, Administrative Affairs,

00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:05.280
Center for International
and Professional Experience,

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:08.170
Strategic Initiatives and Communication,

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:10.110
the Council of Heads of College,

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:12.600
the Residential College Dean's Offices,

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:16.100
Development, External
Affairs and Special Projects,

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:18.350
Finance, Student Affairs,

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:20.770
Student Engagement and
the Cultural Centers,

00:18:20.770 --> 00:18:22.473
and Undergraduate Education.

00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:27.170
Led by fantastic senior
associate deans and directors,

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:29.220
I view each office to be the best

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:31.880
across all of higher education.

00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:33.540
My biggest joy in the deanship

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:36.840
was working with such brilliant
and dedicated colleagues

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:38.790
who are now like a second family to me.

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:41.913
We've been through a lot,

00:18:45.890 --> 00:18:49.340
and my colleagues gave everything
they have, day and night,

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:52.400
without weekends or
recess, to the students,

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:54.450
to each other and me, and to Yale.

00:18:56.980 --> 00:18:59.493
Friends, I really appreciate
you and I love you.

00:19:00.580 --> 00:19:02.120
Dean Lewis, you will love them too.

00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:04.940
The entire Yale College
Dean's office and I

00:19:04.940 --> 00:19:07.170
are thrilled to support your deanship.

00:19:07.170 --> 00:19:10.382
Thank you for accepting the
role and congratulations.

00:19:10.382 --> 00:19:13.549
(audience applauding)

00:19:19.004 --> 00:19:22.004
(indistinct speech)

00:19:27.060 --> 00:19:28.430
- Thank you, Dean Chun.

00:19:28.430 --> 00:19:30.763
We so appreciate your leadership.

00:19:32.130 --> 00:19:36.555
And now I'd like to introduce
the next Dean of Yale College,

00:19:36.555 --> 00:19:37.932
Pericles Lewis.

00:19:37.932 --> 00:19:40.860
(audience applauding)

00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:42.530
- Thank you so much, president Salovey.

00:19:42.530 --> 00:19:45.220
Could I invite those who
are at that end of the room

00:19:45.220 --> 00:19:48.453
to come over into the middle
of the room if you're willing?

00:19:50.680 --> 00:19:52.580
It's good to get a little circulation.

00:19:53.790 --> 00:19:54.623
Thank you.

00:19:56.690 --> 00:19:58.840
Don't worry, I won't
keep you for too long.

00:20:03.350 --> 00:20:06.333
Thank you so much President
Salovey and Dean Chun.

00:20:07.640 --> 00:20:09.940
President Salovey, thank
you for your leadership

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:12.463
and for entrusting this
important role to me.

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:16.080
Thanks also to the members of
the Search Advisory Committee.

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:18.620
I really appreciate your confidence.

00:20:18.620 --> 00:20:19.890
And Dean Chun,

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:22.520
thank you for the inspiring
work that you have done

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:24.000
in the past five years

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:26.010
under some of the difficult circumstances

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:28.710
that you mentioned to lead this community.

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:32.090
It's really a true honor
to be named your successor

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:34.563
and I appreciate your very kind words.

00:20:35.570 --> 00:20:39.390
Next year will be the 25th
since I joined Yale College

00:20:39.390 --> 00:20:41.860
as a faculty member and started teaching.

00:20:41.860 --> 00:20:44.570
The college is a remarkable institution

00:20:44.570 --> 00:20:47.980
dedicated to light and truth.

00:20:47.980 --> 00:20:52.490
And even though we may all
pursue truth by our own lights,

00:20:52.490 --> 00:20:54.200
one of the great things about the college

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:55.860
is the way that we work together,

00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:58.683
we try and help light each other's way.

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:02.830
The fact that we are all
pursuing truth together

00:21:02.830 --> 00:21:04.490
is part of what makes this a place

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:07.860
of great learning and communal joy

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:09.020
and what makes the four years

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:12.100
that our undergraduates spend with us

00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:13.930
so transformative for them

00:21:13.930 --> 00:21:16.380
and also truthfully transformative for us,

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:18.703
the faculty and the staff.

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:22.550
Next academic year will be the fourth year

00:21:22.550 --> 00:21:24.720
since the beginning of the pandemic.

00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:26.170
The class of 2023

00:21:26.170 --> 00:21:31.080
has never known a totally
COVID-free year at Yale.

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:32.250
And these last few years

00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:35.280
have made the central act of Yale College,

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:37.200
which is learning together,

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:40.310
somewhat more challenging,
considerably, more challenging.

00:21:40.310 --> 00:21:44.070
I'd like to salute the
students and the faculty

00:21:44.070 --> 00:21:45.950
and especially the staff

00:21:45.950 --> 00:21:49.640
who have persevered
during challenging times

00:21:49.640 --> 00:21:51.020
and who've made it possible for us

00:21:51.020 --> 00:21:52.810
to continue our educational mission

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:57.810
and also our great research
during this challenging period.

00:21:58.030 --> 00:22:00.920
In particular, I'm very
grateful to the heads

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:03.770
and the deans of the residential colleges,

00:22:03.770 --> 00:22:08.770
to the Yale College Dean's Office staff,

00:22:08.870 --> 00:22:10.880
to the staff of the residential colleges,

00:22:10.880 --> 00:22:12.860
and also of course, to our colleagues

00:22:12.860 --> 00:22:16.640
in hospitality and
facilities, because they've,

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:19.620
all of you together have
kept this place open

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:22.160
and made it possible for students to learn

00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:25.910
even at a time when, at
times it has been challenging

00:22:25.910 --> 00:22:28.780
and when guidelines on public
health have been changing

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:30.830
and sometimes it has been stressful.

00:22:30.830 --> 00:22:31.810
I thank you for that,

00:22:31.810 --> 00:22:34.020
and I hope that this is a an opportunity

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:35.230
for some well deserved rest

00:22:35.230 --> 00:22:39.100
for people who have been working
so hard the last few years.

00:22:39.100 --> 00:22:40.110
I also know that many of you

00:22:40.110 --> 00:22:42.240
have been through a difficult period

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:44.040
and I look forward to supporting the staff

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:47.243
in their essential roles as we go forward.

00:22:49.330 --> 00:22:51.610
I believe that next year
we'll have the opportunity

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:54.510
to recover from the worst of COVID

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:57.620
and to experience the
transformative communal life

00:22:57.620 --> 00:23:00.520
of college together more intensely

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:03.570
in the arts and in athletics,

00:23:03.570 --> 00:23:06.570
in the residential colleges
and the cultural houses,

00:23:06.570 --> 00:23:09.750
in study abroad and volunteer work,

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:12.850
in the library and in the laboratory,

00:23:12.850 --> 00:23:16.970
in the classroom, and in
extracurricular activities.

00:23:16.970 --> 00:23:19.170
And I look forward to
participating in that.

00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:24.920
Next yea will be the 323rd
year of Yale College.

00:23:24.920 --> 00:23:28.130
The college began by educating
young men from Connecticut

00:23:28.130 --> 00:23:31.850
and Western Massachusetts in
the liberal arts and sciences

00:23:31.850 --> 00:23:32.940
in the words of the Charter,

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:36.150
to prepare them for public
employment both in church

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:38.010
and civil state,

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:41.290
and only eight students graduated
at the first commencement

00:23:41.290 --> 00:23:44.590
in New Haven, more than 300 years ago.

00:23:44.590 --> 00:23:47.310
Since then, while the college's goal

00:23:47.310 --> 00:23:49.580
of educating talented young people

00:23:49.580 --> 00:23:52.440
for future leadership has not changed,

00:23:52.440 --> 00:23:54.100
Yale has continually expanded,

00:23:54.100 --> 00:23:56.670
not only in terms of pedagogy,

00:23:56.670 --> 00:23:57.820
not only in terms of numbers,

00:23:57.820 --> 00:24:00.830
but also in the range
of subjects we teach,

00:24:00.830 --> 00:24:05.270
the excellence of our curriculum
and pedagogy and research,

00:24:05.270 --> 00:24:07.680
and the diversity of our student body.

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:10.070
For almost a century,
the residential colleges

00:24:10.070 --> 00:24:12.240
have created enduring communities

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:16.850
that are an essential part of
the broader Yale ecosystem.

00:24:16.850 --> 00:24:20.810
I could not be prouder of the
progress that Yale has made

00:24:20.810 --> 00:24:23.450
in the past 60 years to become a college

00:24:23.450 --> 00:24:25.860
that better reflects the great diversity

00:24:25.860 --> 00:24:27.220
of the United States

00:24:32.950 --> 00:24:35.740
and that welcomes students
regardless of ability to pay

00:24:39.240 --> 00:24:40.433
from around the world.

00:24:42.812 --> 00:24:45.979
(audience applauding)

00:24:53.021 --> 00:24:54.760
There's of course, much more work to do

00:24:54.760 --> 00:24:56.810
and like our sister
schools here and abroad,

00:24:56.810 --> 00:25:01.290
we've faced some tumultuous
times over the last few years

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:03.300
but I believe in a Yale where everyone,

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:07.340
regardless of race or
socioeconomic status,

00:25:07.340 --> 00:25:11.350
gender, sexuality or gender
identity or expression,

00:25:11.350 --> 00:25:13.933
disability or veteran status,

00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:16.670
religious or political perspective

00:25:16.670 --> 00:25:20.003
or national or ethnic
origin, can learn together.

00:25:20.900 --> 00:25:24.590
And such an environment for
learning requires respect

00:25:24.590 --> 00:25:28.610
and tolerance and a willingness
to listen to one another.

00:25:28.610 --> 00:25:30.750
Most of all, it requires an openness

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:32.563
on the part of each of us.

00:25:35.050 --> 00:25:39.593
An openness to learn and a
humility, (sobs) excuse me.

00:25:42.280 --> 00:25:44.530
A humility about how
little we actually know.

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:48.723
I promise I won't do this
every time I make a speech.

00:25:48.723 --> 00:25:51.556
(audience laughs)

00:25:53.110 --> 00:25:56.250
Yale's world leading research,
our dedicated teachers

00:25:56.250 --> 00:25:58.470
our distinctive residential system,

00:25:58.470 --> 00:26:01.580
and what I think of as
the civil society of Yale,

00:26:01.580 --> 00:26:05.520
the web of groups and
traditions and activities

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:07.313
that make up its unique culture,

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:11.423
these are worth cherishing and preserving.

00:26:20.440 --> 00:26:22.260
They've been underpinned over the years

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:24.420
by an ethos of service.

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:25.880
While we of course want our students

00:26:25.880 --> 00:26:28.190
to develop their talents fully

00:26:28.190 --> 00:26:30.870
and their characters as individuals,

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:32.450
we also expect that the Yale Alumni

00:26:32.450 --> 00:26:34.410
will make broader contributions

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:37.330
to the communities that
they choose to live in

00:26:37.330 --> 00:26:40.950
and will use their capacities
to the highest possible ends.

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:42.570
So we hope that our students carry

00:26:42.570 --> 00:26:44.340
the dedication to light and truth

00:26:44.340 --> 00:26:47.213
with them beyond their
four years in the college.

00:26:49.010 --> 00:26:50.560
Thank you again for this honor.

00:26:51.410 --> 00:26:54.610
I take it on in a spirit
of openness and humility.

00:26:54.610 --> 00:26:56.900
In closing, I'd like to thank my family,

00:26:56.900 --> 00:26:58.620
some of whom are with me

00:26:58.620 --> 00:27:00.297
and some of whom are Yale graduates,

00:27:00.297 --> 00:27:01.240
and as was mentioned,

00:27:01.240 --> 00:27:03.753
one of whom is a very
recent Yale graduate indeed.

00:27:05.140 --> 00:27:06.330
I'm grateful to the teams

00:27:06.330 --> 00:27:08.510
in global strategy and
academic initiatives.

00:27:08.510 --> 00:27:10.833
I'm happy to see so many of you here.

00:27:12.900 --> 00:27:15.770
You've worked tirelessly
over the last five years

00:27:15.770 --> 00:27:19.520
to ensure that the mission
of this great university,

00:27:19.520 --> 00:27:22.820
a research university that
as President Salovey has said

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:26.463
puts teaching at its
core, remains central.

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:29.640
To the team I'm joining in Yale College,

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:31.830
I look forward to getting
to know you better,

00:27:31.830 --> 00:27:32.900
to continuing to provide

00:27:32.900 --> 00:27:35.203
the world's greatest
undergraduate education.

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:37.500
To the faculty,

00:27:37.500 --> 00:27:39.810
thank you for your
continued collegial support

00:27:39.810 --> 00:27:41.313
and dedication to our mission.

00:27:42.740 --> 00:27:47.310
To the students, have a wonderful,
exciting restful summer.

00:27:47.310 --> 00:27:51.340
I can't wait to see you all
in the new academic year

00:27:51.340 --> 00:27:55.640
and in the words of the old
Yale football song, Boola Boola.

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:58.390
I probably should have
warned the band about that

00:27:58.390 --> 00:28:01.306
so they could start playing
it, but anyway, boola boola.

00:28:01.306 --> 00:28:02.139
Thank you.

00:28:02.139 --> 00:28:05.306
(audience applauding)

00:28:15.900 --> 00:28:19.740
- Let me just thank Maya and
Sheila for joining us today.

00:28:19.740 --> 00:28:22.980
Once again, Dean Chun, for
your incredible stewardship

00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:24.480
of Yale College,

00:28:24.480 --> 00:28:26.410
and professor Lewis, Pericles Lewis,

00:28:26.410 --> 00:28:29.150
thank you for saying
yes when I called you.

00:28:29.150 --> 00:28:32.050
I think you can all
see, this is a man who,

00:28:32.050 --> 00:28:35.840
whose emotional commitment
to this position is obvious

00:28:35.840 --> 00:28:40.840
and whose passion for the
position is so readily on display.

00:28:44.870 --> 00:28:46.310
We're going to make him wear a mask

00:28:46.310 --> 00:28:48.116
for all future speeches, however.

00:28:48.116 --> 00:28:49.740
(Professor Pericles laughs)

00:28:49.740 --> 00:28:52.670
Thank you all for joining us
today for this announcement.

00:28:52.670 --> 00:28:55.580
We have refreshments,
courtesy of Yale Hospitality.

00:28:55.580 --> 00:29:00.200
We ask you to join us in
celebrating this moment

00:29:00.200 --> 00:29:02.200
by partaking.

00:29:02.200 --> 00:29:03.940
Thank you all for being here

00:29:03.940 --> 00:29:07.075
and thank you all for what
you do for Yale College.

00:29:07.075 --> 00:29:10.325
(audience applauding)

